On 20.06.2011 18:26, stth wrote:
> Viddaric wrote:
> 
>  > In vanilla Nethack, what is, in your opinion, the hardest class to
>  > ascend as, and which is the easiest?
> 
> All roles are easy.
> All roles are hard.

All roles can be easy.
All roles can be hard.

My observation is that you are very dependent on the environment you
have in your actual game. One effect of specific roles is that they
have some more, even crucial options available; dipping for Excalibur
has been mentioned. Why are there so many Wizard start-scummers? I
think it's because of their varying inventory of different usefulness;
some items and item combinations are very good others are just useless
crap. Early shops (for example), or an altar can help a more difficult
role to survive easier. An "empty" dungeon can evolve the game of an
easier role to a nightmare; experienced players have a challenging game,
less experienced players then die easily. - That are just some aspects.
If you now have a sufficiently large base of games, you can collect a
difficulty metric for players from the unskilled to proficient range;
this has been done before; you'll get a typical difficulty distribution
across the roles.

> 
> You can play an "easy" role and die.
> You can play a "hard" role and ascend.
> 
> The key to success in NetHack is to adjust your playing style. Adjust
> to the advantages and disadvantages a role offers. Adjust to what the
> game offers you in general. Most importantly, adjust to the situation
> at hand.

This is very true.

> 
> All of this requires a *lot* of knowledge and experience. And if you
> have finally accumulated enough of those, you will realize something.
> Role doesn't matter. Nothing really matters.

Well, I disagree here on different abstraction levels. For one, I don't
consider difficulty to be a quantity normalized to a very experienced
player; difficulty should have a meaning for beginners with limited
knowledge as well. Difficulty shall tell how easily you can ascend,
whether you can apply a golf bag of tactics and strategies (where it may
matter less; or rather, where you have less effort to compensate problems)
or without large experience and knowledge (the else-thread mentioned "no
clever thinking required").

As a non-beginner I'd say, role matters a lot for beginners, still for
experienced players. But actual "environment" matters more, especially
for non-beginners.

> 
> Adjust. Ascend.

Those who can should do.

